     >>> requirements1 == requirements2
     True
 
+A requirement can be copied with selected fields replaced. This also supports
+``copy.replace()`` on Python 3.13 and later:
+
+.. doctest::
+
+    >>> from packaging.specifiers import SpecifierSet
+    >>> req = Requirement('requests[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')
+    >>> req.__replace__(name="flask")
+    <Requirement('flask[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')>
+    >>> req.__replace__(specifier=SpecifierSet(">=3.0"), marker=None)
+    <Requirement('requests[security]>=3.0')>
+
+The keyword arguments are ``name``, ``url``, ``extras``, ``specifier``, and
+``marker``. Omitted fields are preserved; ``None`` clears a URL or marker,
+while an empty set of extras or an empty ``SpecifierSet`` clears those fields.
+The result is a new instance of the same class, with independent mutable fields.
+Specifier prerelease settings are preserved. Invalid fields, including values
+that would be parsed as different fields, raise :class:`InvalidRequirement`.
+
 .. versionchanged:: 23.2
 
     When a requirement is specified with a URL, the :class:`Requirement` class
